  "description": "Stage W2b: a service's dwell at each call, second by second (x10, one second a tick); since Stage W2c its runs too. Track a(0,1)-b(1,1)-c(2,1)-d(3,1)-e(4,1)-f(5,1)-g(6,1); Alpha(1,0) has platform b, Beta(3,0) d, Gamma(5,0) f. Local (rate 1024) has a crawl, { acceleration 250, braking 250, topSpeed 1 }: two links take at least 4 + 111.2 + 4 s, so 120. It runs Alpha 0/2, Beta 3/3, Gamma 6/7 (minutes), with no passengers. Starting at 0 counts as arriving at Alpha at 0. The doors take 8 s to open: the step at 8 lets passengers off and on (none, so the exchange ends at 8). Alpha is a terminal (the first stop), least dwell 42 s, but the train is early: its doors start closing 9 s before its departure, at 111, and it leaves at 120, on time. Its run to Beta is given the 60 s from 120 to 180, which no curve of the crawl fits, so it takes its least, 120 s (run start 120, length 2048, seconds 120), and reaches Beta at 240 (scheduled 180): 60 s late while it waits. Beta is between the ends, least dwell 36 s: no hold for the timetable, the doors close at 240 + 36 - 9 = 267 and it leaves at 276, 96 s late, which it stays while travelling. Its run to Gamma takes the 180 s its timetable gives it (180 to 360), from that late departure (start 276, length 2048, seconds 180): it reaches Gamma at 456, after its scheduled departure at 420 (lateness 36), and Gamma is the last stop (least dwell 42 s), so its doors close at 456 + 42 - 9 = 489 and the service ends at 498, 78 s late. Each event happens in the step at its second, so a batch that ends at that second has not seen it yet.",
